On SDK 11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128 (`net11.0`), the generator-on run prints the same results as the control.

### Exceptions (if any)

None; the wrong binding is silent.

### .NET Version

10.0.401

### Anything else?

- ASP.NET Core: 10.0.12 (the generator ships in `Microsoft.AspNetCore.App.Ref`)
- No IDE (CLI); Windows 11 (10.0.26200) x64 and Ubuntu x64
- Handlers that differ only by a parameter's default value still share an interceptor on 11.0 as well: #69675.

Found while adding native AOT checks to [AdCodicem.ValueObjects](https://github.com/AdCodicem/AdCodicem.ValueObjects). Investigation and write-up assisted by Claude (Anthropic); the linked repro was run on Windows 11 and Ubuntu, and I reviewed this report.

`MultipartReader.ReadHeadersAsync` accumulates `line.Length` (UTF-16 code units of the decoded header line) to enforce `HeadersLengthLimit`, instead of the raw UTF-8 byte count actually read off the wire:

```csharp
// src/Http/WebUtilities/src/MultipartReader.cs
if (HeadersLengthLimit - totalSize < line.Length)
{
    throw new InvalidDataException(...);
}
totalSize += line.Length;
```

Multi-byte UTF-8 characters undercount here: 2-byte chars (Cyrillic, Greek) ~2x, 3-byte chars (CJK) ~3x worst case, 4-byte chars (emoji) ~2x.

`DefaultHeadersLengthLimit` is documented as "16,384 bytes, approximately 16KB," but with 3-byte UTF-8 header content the actual raw bytes read before the limit trips can go beyond a limit a little bit.

**Suggested fix:** track/accumulate the raw byte length per line (already known internally in `BufferedReadStream`) instead of `string.Length`.

