### Description

`RateLimitingMiddleware` builds a `PartitionedRateLimiter<HttpContext>` for endpoint policies (`_endpointLimiter`, created in its constructor whether or not any policy is used), and keeps the configured `GlobalLimiter` (`_globalLimiter`). The partitioned limiter runs a periodic timer (`DefaultPartitionedRateLimiter.RunTimer`, 100 ms) on the runtime's static timer queue. The middleware is not `IDisposable`/`IAsyncDisposable`, and nothing disposes either limiter when the host or application stops. So once a host has served a request through `UseRateLimiter()`, the timer keeps that host's whole request pipeline, service provider and `WebApplicationFactory` reachable from a static root forever.

- **In production** there is one host per process, so this is a single timer and costs nothing.
- **In an integration-test suite** that creates one `WebApplicationFactory` host per test, every disposed host stays in memory. In a large suite (~2,000 hosts at ~4 MB each) the test process grew linearly with the test count until the 7.9 GB CI runner started swapping. Full GCs then took minutes (`wait_for_bgc_high_memory`), and tests failed with 100 s client timeouts or tripped `--blame-hang`.

### Minimal repro

```csharp
// Program.cs (minimal hosting)
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddRateLimiter(o => o.AddFixedWindowLimiter("p", w => { w.PermitLimit = 10; w.Window = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1); }));
var app = builder.Build();
app.UseRateLimiter();
app.MapGet("/health", () => "ok");
app.Run();
public partial class Program { }

// xunit test
public class RateLimiterLeakTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task DisposedHost_IsCollected()
    {
        var weak = await BuildUseAndDispose();
        for (var i = 0; i < 10 && weak.IsAlive; i++)
        {
            GC.Collect(GC.MaxGeneration, GCCollectionMode.Forced, blocking: true, compacting: true);
            GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();
            await Task.Delay(100);
        }
        Assert.False(weak.IsAlive); // FAILS: the host survives
    }

    [MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.NoInlining)]
    private static async Task<WeakReference> BuildUseAndDispose()
    {
        var factory = new WebApplicationFactory<Program>();
        using (var client = factory.CreateClient())
            (await client.GetAsync("/health")).EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        var weak = new WeakReference(factory.Services);
        await factory.DisposeAsync();
        return weak;
    }
}
```

This fails as written. It passes when `app.UseRateLimiter()` is removed, and it passes with the workaround below (the limiters disposed on factory dispose). All three were run in a standalone project with exactly the code above.

`gcroot` on the disposed host's `DefaultModelMetadataProvider`, from a heap dump taken on Linux (all printed root paths share this shape):

```
static System.Threading.TimerQueue.Instances -> TimerQueueTimer
 -> System.Threading.RateLimiting.TimerAwaitable
 -> DefaultPartitionedRateLimiter<HttpContext, DefaultKeyType>.RunTimer (async state machine box)
 -> ExecutionContext -> AsyncLocal map -> HostFactoryResolver+HostingListener
 -> DeferredHostBuilder -> WebApplicationFactory<Program> -> (the whole host)
```

Suppressing ExecutionContext flow around host creation/start did NOT free the host in our tests. The limiter's partitioner closure also references the middleware and, through `_next`, the rest of the pipeline. Only disposing the limiter did.

### Versions

- Minimal repro verified on: Windows 11, .NET SDK 10.0.303, Microsoft.AspNetCore.App 10.0.11 / Microsoft.NETCore.App 10.0.11, Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing 10.0.9, xunit 2.9.3
- Observed in the full suite on: GitHub-hosted runner ubuntu-24.04 (image 20260920.314.1), .NET SDK 10.0.401, Microsoft.NETCore.App 10.0.12
- aspnetcore source read: `release/10.0`, `src/Middleware/RateLimiting/src/RateLimitingMiddleware.cs`

### Workaround

Verified with the repro above:

```csharp
public sealed class LimiterDisposingFactory : WebApplicationFactory<Program>
{
    private IHost? _host;
    protected override IHost CreateHost(IHostBuilder builder) => _host = base.CreateHost(builder);

    public override async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        var limiters = _host is null ? [] : Find(_host.Services.GetRequiredService<IServer>());
        await base.DisposeAsync();
        foreach (var l in limiters) await l.DisposeAsync();
    }

    // Walk server -> hosting application -> RequestDelegate chain; collect limiters by TYPE.
    private static List<PartitionedRateLimiter<HttpContext>> Find(object server)
    {
        var found = new List<PartitionedRateLimiter<HttpContext>>();
        var seen = new HashSet<object>(ReferenceEqualityComparer.Instance);
        var queue = new Queue<object>([server]);
        while (queue.Count > 0)
        {
            var node = queue.Dequeue();
            if (!seen.Add(node)) continue;
            if (node is PartitionedRateLimiter<HttpContext> l) { found.Add(l); continue; }
            if (node is Delegate d) { foreach (var t in d.GetInvocationList()) if (t.Target is { } o) queue.Enqueue(o); continue; }
            var name = node.GetType().FullName ?? "";
            if (!ReferenceEquals(node, server) && !(name.Contains("Middleware") || name.Contains("<>c__DisplayClass")
                || name.Contains("ApplicationWrapper") || name.Contains("HostingApplication") || name.Contains("Binder") || name.Contains("Closure")))
                continue;
            for (var t = node.GetType(); t is not null; t = t.BaseType)
                foreach (var f in t.GetFields(BindingFlags.Instance | BindingFlags.Public | BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.DeclaredOnly))
                    if (!f.FieldType.IsValueType && f.GetValue(node) is { } v) queue.Enqueue(v);
        }
        return found;
    }
}
```

In words: in the `WebApplicationFactory` subclass, walk the built request pipeline (server → hosting application → `RequestDelegate` chain) and collect every `PartitionedRateLimiter<HttpContext>` **by type**. Collect them before `base.DisposeAsync()` and dispose them after it. This needs reflection into private fields, because the middleware exposes no handle.

A second, bounded root in the same suites: `IHttpClientFactory` arms a 2-minute handler-expiry timer per handler, which keeps a disposed host alive until it fires. `services.ConfigureHttpClientDefaults(b => b.SetHandlerLifetime(Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan))` in the test host removes it. With both workarounds applied, the full suite's peak memory measured locally fell from 9.5 GB to 1.19 GB.

### Ask

Either of:
1. **Dispose the limiters when the application stops.** `RateLimitingMiddleware` (or the rate-limiting services) disposes `_endpointLimiter` and `_globalLimiter` on `IHostApplicationLifetime.ApplicationStopped` / host disposal. The global limiter is user-supplied, so that half may need an opt-in.
2. **Expose a supported handle** to the endpoint limiter (for example a DI-resolvable owner that the container disposes), so a test host can release it without reflection.
